1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,538
In my presentation,

2
00:00:02,538 --> 00:00:05,620
I'm going to talk about coffee

3
00:00:05,620 --> 00:00:10,150
and its importance both in economic and social terms.

4
00:00:10,150 --> 00:00:14,079
We think it was first drunk in the Arab world,

5
00:00:14,079 --> 00:00:18,400
but there's hardly any documentary evidence of it before the 1500s,

6
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:23,900
although of course that doesn't mean that people didn't know about it before then.

7
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:30,988
However, there is evidence that coffee was originally gathered from bushes growing wild in Ethiopia,

8
00:00:30,988 --> 00:00:33,350
in the northeast of Africa.

9
00:00:33,350 --> 00:00:35,524
In the early sixteenth century,

10
00:00:35,524 --> 00:00:37,960
it was being bought by traders

11
00:00:38,520 --> 00:00:43,350
and gradually its use as a drink spread throughout the Middle East.

12
00:00:43,350 --> 00:00:48,771
It's also known that in 1522, in the Turkish city of Constantinople,

13
00:00:48,771 --> 00:00:52,160
which was the centre of the Ottoman Empire

14
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:56,450
the court physician approved its use as a medicine.

15
00:00:56,450 --> 00:01:01,258
By the mid-1500s, coffee bushes were being cultivated in the Yemen

16
00:01:01,258 --> 00:01:04,040
and for the next hundred years

17
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:10,000
this region produced most of the coffee drunk in Africa and the Arab world.

18
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,850
What's particularly interesting about coffee is its effect on social life.

19
00:01:15,850 --> 00:01:22,450
It was rarely drunk at home, but instead people went to coffee houses to drink it.

20
00:01:22,450 --> 00:01:26,760
These people, usually men, would meet to drink coffee

21
00:01:26,760 --> 00:01:29,550
and chat about issues of the day.

22
00:01:29,550 --> 00:01:33,300
But at the time, this chance to share ideas

23
00:01:33,300 --> 00:01:38,837
and opinions was seen as something that was potentially dangerous,

24
00:01:38,837 --> 00:01:47,635
and in 1623 the ruler of Constantinople demanded the destruction of all the coffee houses in the city,

25
00:01:47,635 --> 00:01:53,500
although after his death many new ones opened, and coffee consumption continued.

26
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:57,933
In the seventeenth century, coffee drinking spread to Europe,

27
00:01:57,933 --> 00:02:02,400
and here too coffee shops became places where ordinary people

28
00:02:02,760 --> 00:02:06,800
nearly always men, could meet to exchange ideas.

29
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:14,400
Because of this, some people said that these places performed a similar function to universities.

30
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:20,095
The opportunity they provided for people to meet together outside their own homes

31
00:02:20,095 --> 00:02:26,667
and to discuss the topics of the day had an enormous impact on social life,

32
00:02:26,667 --> 00:02:32,800
and many social movements and political developments had their origins in coffee house discussions.

33
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:38,704
In the late 1600s, the Yemeni monopoly on coffee production broke down

34
00:02:38,704 --> 00:02:45,100
and coffee production started to spread around the world, helped by European colonisation.

35
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:48,833
Europeans set up coffee plantations in Indonesia

36
00:02:48,833 --> 00:02:54,700
and the Caribbean and production of coffee in the colonies skyrocketed.

37
00:02:54,700 --> 00:02:58,276
Different types of coffee were produced in different areas,

38
00:02:58,276 --> 00:03:02,646
and it's interesting that the names given to these different types,

39
00:03:02,646 --> 00:03:04,900
like Mocha or Java coffee

40
00:03:05,320 --> 00:03:09,400
were often taken from the port they were shipped to Europe from.

41
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:12,700
But if you look at the labour system in the different colonies

42
00:03:13,020 --> 00:03:15,700
there were some significant differences.

43
00:03:15,700 --> 00:03:19,267
In Brazil and the various Caribbean colonies,

44
00:03:19,267 --> 00:03:26,400
coffee was grown in huge plantations and the workers there were almost all slaves.

45
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:29,276
But this wasn't the same in all colonies;

46
00:03:29,276 --> 00:03:34,669
for example in Java, which had been colonised by the Dutch, the peasants grew coffee

47
00:03:34,669 --> 00:03:37,980
and passed a proportion of this on to the Dutch

48
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:41,500
so it was used as a means of taxation.

49
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:47,721
But whatever system was used, under the European powers of the eighteenth century,

50
00:03:47,721 --> 00:03:51,550
coffee production was very closely linked to colonisation.

51
00:03:51,550 --> 00:03:57,445
Coffee was grown in ever-increasing quantities to satisfy the growing demand from Europe,

52
00:03:57,445 --> 00:04:01,320
and it became nearly as important as sugar production

53
00:04:01,740 --> 00:04:04,700
which was grown under very similar conditions.

54
00:04:04,700 --> 00:04:07,360
However, coffee prices were not

55
00:04:07,360 --> 00:04:10,967
yet low enough for people to drink it regularly at home,

56
00:04:10,967 --> 00:04:15,275
so most coffee consumption still took place in public coffee houses

57
00:04:15,275 --> 00:04:18,800
and it still remained something of a luxury item.

58
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:22,771
In Britain, however, a new drink was introduced from China,

59
00:04:22,771 --> 00:04:26,743
and started to become popular, gradually taking over from coffee,

60
00:04:26,743 --> 00:04:32,700
although at first it was so expensive that only the upper classes could afford it.

61
00:04:32,700 --> 00:04:39,550
This was tea, and by the late 1700s it was being widely drunk.

62
00:04:39,550 --> 00:04:45,220
However, when the USA gained independence from Britain in 1776

63
00:04:45,780 --> 00:04:46,937
they identified this drink with Britain,

64
00:04:46,937 --> 00:04:53,400
and coffee remained the preferred drink in the USA, as it still is today.

65
00:04:53,400 --> 00:04:56,540
So, by the early nineteenth century

66
00:04:56,900 --> 00:05:00,550
coffee was already being widely produced and consumed.

67
00:05:00,550 --> 00:05:07,000
But during this century, production boomed and coffee prices started to fall.

68
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:11,440
This was partly because new types of transportation had been developed

69
00:05:11,440 --> 00:05:14,150
which were cheaper and more efficient.

70
00:05:14,150 --> 00:05:17,720
So now, working people could afford to buy coffee

71
00:05:18,220 --> 00:05:21,000
it wasn't just a drink for the middle classes.

72
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:22,680
And this was at a time

73
00:05:22,680 --> 00:05:26,650
when large parts of Europe were starting to work in industries.

74
00:05:26,650 --> 00:05:31,360
And sometimes this meant their work didn't stop when it got dark:

75
00:05:31,360 --> 00:05:34,500
they might have to continue throughout the night.

76
00:05:34,500 --> 00:05:38,227
So, the use of coffee as a stimulant became important -

77
00:05:38,227 --> 00:05:42,700
it wasn't just a drink people drank in the morning, for breakfast.

78
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:46,300
There were also changes in cultivation...
